Exception handling is a mechanism to handle runtime errors gracefully without crashing the program.

TRY-CATCH-FINALLY BLOCKS:

TRY:
• Contains code that might throw an exception
• If exception occurs, control jumps to catch block

CATCH:
• Handles specific exception types
• Can have multiple catch blocks
• Catches exceptions in order (specific to general)

FINALLY:
• Executes regardless of exception
• Used for cleanup (close files, connections)
• Optional block

Syntax:
try {
    int result = 10 / 0; // May throw exception
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("General exception");
} finally {
    System.out.println("Always executes");
}

COMMON EXCEPTIONS:
• NullPointerException: Accessing null object
• ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Invalid array index
• ArithmeticException: Division by zero
• IOException: File/IO operations

BENEFITS:
1. Prevents program crashes
2. Graceful error handling
3. Better user experience
4. Helps in debugging
